writeDoc({
  title: "The BCI Installation — A Mother's Account",
  category: "Daily Life",
  body: `# The BCI Installation — A Mother's Account

## She Is Six Years Old

The waiting room at Shelf Medical Station 14 smells like antiseptic and anxiety. There are eleven other families here. Every child is six, because six is the age — the governance compact mandates BCI installation between the sixth and seventh birthday, and the Shelf's medical stations process them in batches, fifteen per day, Tuesday through Saturday. My daughter sits beside me on a polymer chair that is too large for her. Her feet do not reach the floor. She is swinging them. She does not know what is about to happen to her, not really. I have explained it in the terms the orientation packet suggests: "A small computer that helps you talk to the world." She asked if it would hurt. I said no. The packet says the procedure is painless under local anesthesia. The packet does not describe the sound.

They call her name at 10:47 AM. We walk down a corridor that is clean in the way that only medical facilities are clean — aggressively, reproachfully clean, as if dirt were a personal failing. The procedure room is small. A reclining chair that adjusts to child proportions. A display screen showing a cartoon — the same cartoon that plays in every BCI installation room in the city, a proprietary Ringo production about a friendly robot that gets a new brain. My daughter watches the cartoon. I watch the technician. He is young, perhaps twenty-five, and his hands are steady and his manner is professional and kind. He explains each step as he performs it. I hear approximately one word in three because the rest are lost in the sound of my own heartbeat.

The procedure takes twenty minutes. The local anesthesia is applied to the base of the skull with a pneumatic injector that makes a sound like a stapler. My daughter flinches but does not cry. The technician makes a small incision — I cannot see it from where I sit, and I am grateful. The BCI unit itself is the size of a grain of rice, delivered through a needle that penetrates the skin, navigates through the subcutaneous layer, and seats the device against the neural interface point at the brainstem. The needle makes a sound when it punctures — a small, precise click that I will hear in my sleep for weeks. My daughter says, "Oh." Not pain. Surprise. The feeling of something arriving in a place where nothing has ever been.

The activation sequence takes ninety seconds. The technician runs a diagnostic — a series of tones played through the BCI's internal speakers, each one calibrated to test a different neural pathway. My daughter's face changes with each tone. Not dramatically. Subtle shifts in expression that I have never seen before — the face of a person receiving information through a channel that did not exist three minutes ago. Her eyes move in a pattern that is not tracking anything in the room. She is seeing the feed for the first time. The augmented reality overlay that will be her constant companion for the rest of her life is booting up, painting her visual field with data, labels, directions, advertisements, notifications. The world she was born into is being replaced, in real time, by the world she will live in.

The moment. There is a moment. Every parent who has been through this describes it, and no parent describes it the same way. My daughter's eyes focus. Not on me, not on the technician, not on the cartoon robot. On something between us and not between us — something that exists in her visual field and not in mine. Her expression is — I don't have the word. Wonder is too simple. Confusion is too negative. It is the face of a person encountering a new dimension. She looks at me, and I know — I know with the certainty that only a parent possesses — that she is seeing me differently. My face, annotated. My name, floating beside my head. My Quanta balance, my medical history, my social connections, all the data that the BCI makes available rendered as an overlay on the face of her mother. She smiles. "Mama," she says, "you're blue." I don't know what this means. I won't know for months, until I learn that the BCI's default color-coding system assigns blue to family members. My daughter's first augmented perception of me was a color. I was blue. I am still, in her interface, blue. I will be blue until she changes the setting or until I die, and I don't know which of these I find more unbearable.`,
  tags: ["document", "daily_life", "bci", "children", "installation", "medical", "shelf", "mother", "augmentation", "meridian", "texture", "family"]
});

writeDoc({
  title: "Maintenance Day",
  category: "Daily Life",
  body: `# Maintenance Day

## Getting Your Augmentations Serviced at a Shelf Clinic

The sign says CHROME CHECK — WALK-INS WELCOME, but the wait time written on the board beneath it says 2:40, which means two hours and forty minutes, and the twelve people sitting in the waiting room say that the board is optimistic. The clinic occupies the ground floor of Block 9's residential tower, a space that was designed as a retail unit and has been converted with the minimum investment necessary to meet health governance standards. The walls are clinic-white polymer over retail-beige concrete. The floor is scuffed by a decade of augmented feet. The chairs are the same injection-molded polymer found in every waiting room in the Shelf — rated for 120 kilograms, which is insufficient for any patient with bilateral leg augmentations, which is why the corner bench is reinforced with welded scrap metal and reserved for the heavily chromed.

The intake process is automated and personal simultaneously. Your BCI handshakes with the clinic's diagnostic system the moment you cross the threshold, uploading your augmentation registry, your maintenance history, your power cell data, and your payment tier. The system knows what you have, when it was installed, and when it was last serviced. It calculates a priority score based on urgency, and if your power cells are below 20% or your joint calibration has drifted beyond tolerance, you jump the queue. Everyone understands this. Nobody resents it. Having your arm stop working in the waiting room is the only thing worse than waiting.

The mechanic — they call themselves technicians, but the Shelf calls them mechanics, and the Shelf is right because the work is mechanical in the truest sense — is a woman named Fen. She is fifty-two, unaugmented, and her hands are the most precise instruments in the clinic. The diagnostic suite uses sensors and algorithms. Fen uses touch. She grips your chrome hand and feels the play in the joints. She flexes your wrist and listens for the specific grinding that means bearing wear versus the clicking that means actuator misalignment. She presses the interface zone where chrome meets flesh and watches your face, because the flinch tells her about nerve impingement that the sensors miss. The diagnostic suite is faster. Fen is better. Both are used. The suite for documentation. Fen for truth.

The tests are a catalogue of what you are. Grip strength: the chrome hand crushes a dynamometer while the display shows newtons and the mechanic watches for asymmetry between the commanded force and the delivered force. Reflex response: a sensor tap on the joint while the BCI measures latency between the nerve signal and the actuator response — healthy is under 15 milliseconds, concerning is 15 to 25, and over 25 means the neural interface is degrading. Power cell capacity: a full discharge-charge cycle that takes forty minutes and tells you how much of your arm's original battery life remains. The number only goes down. Every maintenance visit is a reminder that your augmentation is aging, that the chrome you carry is not permanent but borrowed, that one day the power cell will hold too little charge to be useful and you will need a replacement you probably cannot afford.

The bill arrives on your BCI as you walk out the door. The clinic charges Φ8 for a standard maintenance check, Φ15 for bearing replacement, Φ25 for actuator recalibration, and Φ120 for a power cell swap. Your daily Quanta allocation is Φ12. The math is the same math that governs everything in the Shelf — technically possible, practically crushing. You pay the Φ8. You will come back when the bearings fail. You will find the Φ15 somewhere. You will not think about the power cell until you have to, and when you have to, you will think about nothing else. Fen watches you leave. She has seen this calculation performed on a thousand faces. She does not say what she knows, which is that the maintenance interval recommended by the manufacturer is every six months, and her average patient comes in every fourteen, because the math only works if you skip every other visit and hope that the chrome holds. The chrome usually holds. Usually is the most expensive word in the Shelf.`,
  tags: ["document", "daily_life", "cyberware", "maintenance", "clinic", "shelf", "chrome", "augmentation", "mechanic", "quanta", "medical", "texture"]
});

writeDoc({
  title: "The Evening Feed",
  category: "Daily Life",
  body: `# The Evening Feed

## What People Consume Through Their BCIs After Work

The transition happens at approximately 6:30 PM, give or take the commute. You walk through the door of your Shelf apartment, and the BCI senses the shift — the familiar electromagnetic signature of home, the drop in ambient noise, the change in posture as your body surrenders the rigid efficiency of the workday. The feed adjusts. The informational overlay — work schedules, task lists, navigation data — fades, replaced by the softer palette of entertainment mode. The BCI's display dims to warm tones. The audio processing shifts from speech-priority to immersion-priority. You are no longer a worker. You are now a consumer. The transition is seamless, automatic, and so familiar that you do not notice the moment you stop being one thing and become another.

The content arrives in layers. The first layer is ambient — a neural soundscape that fills the auditory cortex with something between music and silence, designed to lower cortisol and elevate dopamine just enough to make sitting still feel like a choice rather than a collapse. The second layer is narrative — a serialized story that has been running for weeks, its plot calibrated by algorithm to your emotional baseline, its characters shaped by your engagement patterns, its cliffhangers timed to your attention span. The third layer is social — a feed of other people's evenings, curated to show lives that are slightly better than yours in ways that are aspirational rather than depressing, because the algorithm has learned that envy drives engagement and despair drives disconnection.

The ads are not ads. They are suggestions. The BCI delivers them as neural nudges — not images or sounds but impulses. A slight craving for something sweet. A vague dissatisfaction with your current furniture. An impulse to check the price of a product you have never consciously considered. The sponsoring corponation pays for this access to your neural pathways, and the payment subsidizes your Tier 1 entertainment feed, which is why Tier 1 entertainment is free and Tier 1 residents are the most advertised-to demographic in the city. You are not watching ads. You are being gently, persistently shaped by them, and the shaping feels like your own preferences because it is delivered through the same neural channels as your actual preferences. The distinction between want and suggestion blurs. This is the product.

The numbness arrives at approximately the ninety-minute mark. You have been consuming content for an hour and a half. You have not moved. Your body has settled into the specific posture of feed-consumption — spine curved, shoulders rounded, face slack, eyes unfocused because the content is being delivered directly to the visual cortex and the actual eyes are unnecessary. The BCI tracks your engagement metrics and notes the decline — attention wavering, emotional response flattening, the specific neural signature of a person who has been passively consuming long enough to stop processing. The algorithm responds by increasing stimulation: a plot twist, a social notification, a louder neural nudge. You re-engage. The cycle restarts. Ninety minutes later, it will happen again.

The moment of realization happens at approximately 10:30 PM. You blink. The room is dark — you didn't notice the sun setting because the BCI compensates for ambient light. Your dinner is uneaten, or eaten without memory of eating it, the nutrient paste tube empty on the table beside you. Four hours have passed. Four hours of content that you chose and did not choose, of stories that engaged you and did not satisfy you, of advertisements that became desires that became expenditures recorded on your Quanta account. You feel nothing in particular. Not rested. Not entertained. Not unhappy. Just emptied. The feed has given you everything it has and taken everything you had — your evening, your attention, your solitude — and the exchange feels fair only because you cannot remember what you would have done with those four hours if the feed had not claimed them. This is the evening. This is every evening. The BCI dims to sleep mode. Tomorrow the feed will remember what you watched and offer more of the same, and you will accept, because the alternative is the silence, and the silence is the room, and the room is the Shelf, and the Shelf is your life, and the feed is the thing that makes your life bearable by replacing it with something else.`,
  tags: ["document", "daily_life", "bci", "entertainment", "feed", "advertising", "shelf", "neural", "evening", "meridian", "surveillance", "texture"]
});

writeDoc({
  title: "Shelf Laundry",
  category: "Daily Life",
  body: `# Shelf Laundry

## The Communal Washing Machines

The laundry room in Block 7, Level 3, contains four washing machines, two dryers, and the social infrastructure of an entire residential floor. The machines are industrial models repurposed from a Grind fabrication plant, over-engineered for the task of washing clothes and under-maintained by a building management system that prioritizes structural integrity over comfort amenities. Machine 2 has not spun properly since October. Machine 4 makes a sound during the rinse cycle that resembles a maglev train decoupling. Machines 1 and 3 work. This means that twenty-eight residential units share two functional washing machines, and the schedule — maintained on a shared BCI calendar by communal agreement — allocates forty-five-minute slots that are coveted, traded, and occasionally the subject of disputes that Mrs. Kwan is called upon to adjudicate.

The schedule runs from 5:00 AM to 11:00 PM, eighteen hours, twenty-four slots per machine, forty-eight total slots per day for twenty-eight units. The math works if every household does laundry twice a week. The math does not account for children, who generate laundry at a rate that defies physics, or for the augmented, whose interface zones produce a discharge during healing that stains fabric in ways that require hot water and extra cycles. Slot-trading is the laundry room's shadow economy. A morning slot — the preferred time, when the machines are least likely to break down — can be traded for an evening slot plus a favor: a container of water from the recycling station, an hour of childcare, a look the other way when someone's maintenance fees are late. The economy is informal, unrecorded, and more efficient than Ringo's allocation algorithms.

The theft is petty and persistent. Clothes left in a machine past the end of a slot will be removed by the next user — this is expected and accepted, the social contract of shared infrastructure. But clothes left in the common folding area will occasionally vanish, particularly children's clothes, which are perpetually in short supply, and thermal wraps, which are valuable enough to resell. The community has developed countermeasures: a buddy system where neighbors watch each other's laundry, a BCI-based alert that notifies you when your cycle completes, and the specific social sanction of Mrs. Kwan, who once identified a laundry thief by the distinctive coffee stain on a stolen thermal wrap and delivered a public reprimand so thorough that the thief moved to Block 9.

The conversations in the laundry room are different from the conversations in the water queue. The water queue is anxious — will there be enough? The laundry room is domestic — this stain won't come out, this machine is dying, my kid outgrew these pants in a month. The room smells like detergent and warm polymer and the specific chemical scent of recycled water being heated. People fold clothes on a shared table that is too small for the purpose, their hands moving in the automatic rhythms of domestic labor while their mouths share the news that BCIs don't carry — who is sick, who is pregnant, who got hired at the Circuit fabrication plant, who got their augmentation repossessed for missed payments.

Clean clothes feel like a luxury because they are. When you own two outfits — a work set and a non-work set, both fabricated from the cheapest polymer-blend textile, both fading to the same indeterminate gray — the forty-five minutes that they spend in a washing machine transforms them into something that feels disproportionately valuable. The warmth of fabric fresh from the dryer. The slight stiffness of detergent-clean cloth against your skin. The smell — clean laundry has a smell that is universal across centuries and economic tiers, the one olfactory democracy that the Shelf still participates in. You press your face into a warm, clean shirt, and for three seconds you are not a Tier 1 resident of Block 7. You are just a person with clean clothes. The three seconds end. You fold the shirt carefully and carry it home and wear it tomorrow, and the day after, and the day after that, until the forty-five-minute slot comes around again and the cycle restarts.`,
  tags: ["document", "daily_life", "laundry", "shelf", "community", "mundane", "routine", "poverty", "meridian", "texture"]
});

writeDoc({
  title: "The Sound of a Shelf Night",
  category: "Daily Life",
  body: `# The Sound of a Shelf Night

## What You Hear Through Thin Walls

Midnight in Block 7, Level 12. The building has settled into its nighttime register — a lower, slower version of its daytime self, the mechanical systems throttled down, the human systems winding toward rest. But rest in the Shelf is a relative term, and the sounds that fill the gap between waking and sleeping are as layered and complex as anything the day produces. You lie in the dark and you listen, because listening is the one sense the walls do not attenuate.

The plumbing speaks first. The shared water system runs through the building like a circulatory system, and its sounds are diagnostic — a healthy flow is a low, even hiss; a pressure fluctuation produces a staccato hammering that travels five floors; a blockage somewhere below creates a gurgling that sounds disconcertingly organic, as if the building's digestive system is disagreeing with its last meal. At 12:30 AM, someone on Level 10 flushes a toilet, and the event propagates upward through the pipe stack as a rush-and-clank that is as familiar as breathing and twice as annoying.

Through the wall to your left, Unit 23: someone's BCI is playing audio through external speakers. This means their neural interface is glitching — the internal audio pathway has failed, and the BCI has defaulted to its backup output, a small speaker embedded in the mastoid bone that was designed for diagnostics and was never meant to serve as entertainment delivery. The sound is thin, tinny, and recognizable as a serialized drama — one of the popular BCI narratives, its emotional orchestration reduced to a mosquito-frequency monologue leaking through the wall. The listener cannot hear how bad it sounds. They are probably asleep, the BCI running on its own momentum, playing content to a consciousness that has checked out for the night.

Outside, the automaton street cleaner passes at 2:14 AM. It follows the same route every night, and its sound signature is a mechanical Morse code — the whir of rotary brushes, the suction of its vacuum intake, the clank of its collection bin being repositioned at each turn. The cleaner is not sentient. It has no opinion about the Shelf or its cleanliness or the specific nature of the debris it collects at two in the morning. But its regularity has made it a landmark in the night's soundscape, and residents who hear it know, without checking a clock, approximately what time it is. 2:14 AM. Everything is functioning. The city is maintaining itself.

At 3:07 AM, a dog barks. The sound is so rare that it cuts through every other layer of the nightscape and arrives in your consciousness with the clarity of an alarm. Dogs are rare in the Shelf. Dogs are rare everywhere in Meridian 88. The bark is sharp, urgent, coming from the courtyard below — a real animal, making a real sound, in a world where most sounds are manufactured, processed, or mediated. The bark comes again. Then a voice, soft and urgent: "Shhh. Shhh, baby, quiet." The dog subsides. The voice subsides. The night resumes. But the silence after the bark is different from the silence before it — it has been interrupted by something alive, something that does not run on power cells or neural interfaces, something that barked because it wanted to bark, and the memory of that sound will outlast the night in which it was made.

At some point after 3:30, someone in the building cries. It is muffled, distant, coming from above or below — impossible to localize through the building's complex acoustic geometry. It is not dramatic crying. Not sobs. Just the quiet, effortful weeping of a person who is trying not to be heard and who is being heard by everyone. The Shelf holds its collective breath. No one goes to the door. No one calls out. No one messages on the BCI. This, too, is mercy — the same mercy as the averted eyes when someone's Quanta balance hits zero. The gift of pretending you do not hear what everyone hears. The person cries. The building listens. The night continues. By morning, the crying will be folded into the general noise of the Shelf's existence, one more sound in a building made of sounds, and no one will mention it because mentioning it would require acknowledging that the walls are thin enough to hear through, and acknowledging that would require admitting that privacy in the Shelf is a fiction, and admitting that would make it impossible to live here. So no one admits it. And everyone lives here. And the night sounds continue, and the morning will come, and the water will come on at 5:00 AM, and the walls will still be thin, and the living will go on.`,
  tags: ["document", "daily_life", "sound", "night", "shelf", "community", "bci", "automata", "dog", "privacy", "meridian", "texture"]
});

writeDoc({
  title: "How You Sleep",
  category: "Daily Life",
  body: `# How You Sleep

## The Specific Experience of Sleeping with a BCI

The BCI does not turn off. This is the first thing you learn after installation and the last thing you accept. The device that lives at the base of your skull, threaded into your neural pathways, processing every waking moment into data and overlay and connection — it does not have an off switch. It has a sleep mode. Sleep mode dims the augmented reality overlay, suspends non-critical notifications, reduces the feed to a low-bandwidth ambient state, and shifts the processing priority from external stimulation to internal maintenance. But it does not turn off. Even in your deepest sleep, the BCI is awake, monitoring your vital signs, maintaining its connection to the network, processing the electromagnetic environment around you. You sleep. It does not.

The transition to sleep is a specific experience that has no pre-BCI equivalent. As your consciousness dims, the overlay dims with it — the data labels, the navigation markers, the social annotations that populate your visual field during waking hours dissolve one by one, like stars fading at dawn. But they do not all fade at the same rate. Priority notifications persist longer than ambient data. A low Quanta balance warning will glow faintly behind your closing eyelids for minutes after everything else has gone dark. An unread message from a flagged contact will pulse with a gentle insistence that your drowsy brain processes as a sense of unfinished business. The last thing to fade is the clock — the time display that the BCI maintains in the upper-right corner of your visual field, blinking away only when your neural activity drops below the consciousness threshold. Many people discover that they know exactly when they fall asleep, because the last thing they see is the time.

Feed-dreams are the phenomenon that no one anticipated and everyone experiences. During REM sleep, the BCI's ambient feed — the low-bandwidth content stream that continues during sleep mode — interacts with the brain's dream-generation processes to produce a hybrid experience: dreams that are partly organic and partly fed. You dream of your mother's kitchen, but the kitchen has product placement. You dream of flying, but the sky contains the gentle suggestion of a Ringo promotional offer. You dream of the dead, and the dead have BCI data labels floating beside their names. The content is not intrusive — the BCI's sleep mode is designed to minimize stimulation — but it is present, woven into the fabric of your unconscious experience like a watermark on paper. You cannot dream without the feed, because the feed has become part of the architecture of your mind.

The 3 AM notifications are a known issue. The BCI's notification system is programmed to suppress non-critical alerts during sleep mode, but the definition of "non-critical" is set by the content providers, not by the user. A Ringo inventory alert is classified as non-critical. A Quanta balance change is classified as non-critical. But a sponsored notification from a premium advertiser — one who has paid for sleep-tier access — is classified as priority, because priority classification is a purchasable tier. These notifications arrive as dreams. Not as intrusions — the BCI is more subtle than that — but as inflections in ongoing dream content. A dream about water becomes a dream about the specific mineral water that the advertiser sells. A dream about warmth becomes a dream about the heating system the advertiser manufactures. You do not wake. You do not know, in the morning, that your dream was sponsored. You just know that you are thirsty, or cold, and that a specific product comes to mind. This is the cost of free-tier sleep.

The difference between Tier 1 sleep and Tier 5 sleep is documented in the Meridian 88 Health Governance Board's annual report, though the Board does not describe it in these terms. Tier 5 BCIs run premium sleep mode: zero ambient feed, zero sponsored content, full notification suppression, and an active dream-optimization protocol that monitors sleep architecture and makes real-time adjustments to neural processing to maximize restorative sleep. Tier 5 residents sleep deeply, dream cleanly, and wake rested. Tier 1 BCIs run basic sleep mode: low-bandwidth ambient feed, sleep-tier advertising, partial notification suppression, and no dream optimization. Tier 1 residents sleep shallowly, dream commercially, and wake with the specific fatigue of a mind that has been working through the night — processing content, receiving impressions, generating value for advertisers — while the body it inhabits lay motionless and believed itself at rest. The gap between tiers is measured in hours, in cognitive clarity, in the quality of the consciousness that greets the morning. It is the most intimate inequality in Meridian 88. It happens inside your skull. You cannot close a door against it.`,
  tags: ["document", "daily_life", "sleep", "bci", "dreams", "advertising", "shelf", "spires", "tier_system", "neural", "meridian", "surveillance", "texture"]
});

